= Logic — Technical =

== Formal structure ==
A logic can be represented by:

<pre>
L = formal language
⊢ = syntactic derivability relation
⊨ = semantic consequence relation
</pre>

A proof system is '''sound''' when:

<pre>
Γ ⊢ φ  ⇒  Γ ⊨ φ
</pre>

and '''complete''' when:

<pre>
Γ ⊨ φ  ⇒  Γ ⊢ φ
</pre>

These properties are system-relative.

== Consequence ==
Logical consequence can be characterized proof-theoretically, model-theoretically, algebraically, or inferentially.

Different logics may validate different consequence relations.

== Structural properties ==
Consequence relations can satisfy properties such as reflexivity, monotonicity, transitivity/cut, compactness, or substitution invariance.

Nonmonotonic logics deliberately reject some properties of classical consequence.

== Metalogic ==
Metalogic studies properties of logical systems themselves, including consistency, completeness, decidability, expressiveness, and model-theoretic behavior.

